VERDICT: FAIL
CHECKER: checker-second
FAMILY: adversarial
TASK: T6
ATTEMPT: 3
---
Attack 1 (sweep every live write path under internal/ and cmd/ that can land in data/) FOUND A COUNTEREXAMPLE — this alone is fatal to the enumeration claim.

README ~line 84 (attempt 3, current):
"The files you keep in `data/` need to be real files, not symlinks. When
SimpleBudget saves one of your files, it does not follow a symlink there:
it either replaces the link with a regular file ... or refuses to write
over it. The one exception is the app's own download cache (`data/cache/`),
which it writes in place, so don't rely on symlinking that either."

This claims exactly two behaviors (replace-the-link / refuse) plus exactly
one disclosed exception (data/cache/). A third, undisclosed, LIVE exception
exists: internal/services/storage/migration.go writes two files directly
into s.baseDir (== c.DataDirectory, the same "data/" the README is talking
about) with bare os.WriteFile, which follows a symlink at the destination
instead of replacing or refusing:

  internal/services/storage/migration.go:44-48
    verifyPath := filepath.Join(s.baseDir, verifyFile)   // ".encryption-verify"
    ...
    os.WriteFile(verifyPath, encrypted, 0644)

  internal/services/storage/migration.go:100-102
    markerPath := filepath.Join(s.baseDir, markerFile)   // ".encrypted"
    os.WriteFile(markerPath, []byte("encrypted"), 0644)

Neither goes through storage.atomicWrite's stage+rename, and neither is
data/cache/.

Reachability (live, unconditional, not behind a feature flag or dead code
like attempt 1's SaveUserSettings):
  cmd/server/main.go:301  backup.RegisterRoutes(r)          (registered inside
                                                              the lock-check
                                                              middleware group)
  internal/handlers/backup/handlers.go:110
    r.Post("/encryption/enable", HandleEnableEncryptionWithMethod)
  HandleEnableEncryptionWithMethod → default case →
    handleEnablePasswordEncryption (line ~856) →
    store.EnableEncryption(password) → EnableEncryptionWithProvider
    (migration.go:24) → the two os.WriteFile calls above.
  So POST /encryption/enable with no method (or method=password), the
  default encryption setup path, hits both writes.

Reproduction (mirrors migration.go:44-48 exactly, run in
/tmp/t6repro/repro.go):
  - created data/.encryption-verify as a symlink to an external file
    outside.txt containing "ORIGINAL-CONTENT-OUTSIDE"
  - called os.WriteFile(verifyPath, []byte("ENCRYPTED-VERIFY-BLOB"), 0644)
    (the literal statement at migration.go:49)
  - result:
      verifyPath is still a symlink after write: true
      outside.txt content after write: ENCRYPTED-VERIFY-BLOB
  i.e. the symlink was NOT replaced with a regular file (contradicting arm
  1 of the README sentence), and the write was NOT refused (contradicting
  arm 2) — it silently followed the link and overwrote the link's target,
  the same failure mode the task's own HandlePlotly comment describes for
  data/cache/, but for a file the README does not list as an exception.

Is ".encryption-verify"/".encrypted" a "file you keep in data/"? They are
hidden files the app itself creates directly inside the data directory
(same directory the README addresses as a whole — "the files you keep in
`data/` need to be real files"), not user content, but also not the one
named exception (data/cache/). Whether or not a user would call these
"their" files, the sentence's own structure — "it either replaces or
refuses ... the ONE exception is data/cache/" — is falsified: there are at
least two exceptions in the running server, and the README lists one.
This is exactly the class of gap the task instructions called out
(un-swept write path with the same follow-symlink failure mode as
HandlePlotly, silently uncovered by the enumeration).

Other paths swept and cleared (no further live counterexamples found):
  - internal/services/storage/auth.go saveConfig: tmp+rename, OK.
  - internal/services/storage/age_provider.go: os.OpenFile(O_CREATE|
    O_WRONLY|O_EXCL) — refuses on existing destination (including a
    symlink target that already exists), consistent with "refuses" arm;
    also identityPath is a user-supplied arbitrary path, not necessarily
    under data/.
  - internal/services/backup/snapshot.go buildZip: os.OpenFile(O_EXCL) into
    a fresh tmp name, then rename — refuses/atomic, OK; cleanOrphanTmp only
    os.Remove()s stale *.tmp.
  - internal/services/backup/service.go saveEnabled: tmp+rename, OK.
  - internal/services/backup/meta.go writeMetaAtomic: tmp+rename, OK.
  - internal/services/mcpsvc/snapshot/snapshot.go Ensure: os.WriteFile to a
    freshly-timestamped filename (name+timestamp+".bak") — bare write, not
    stage+rename, and not the cache exception either; in practice the
    destination name is fresh per call so a pre-existing symlink there is
    an unlikely setup, but the code is technically a second/third
    unenumerated bare-write path into an app-managed subdirectory. Recorded
    as a secondary observation, not the basis of this FAIL (the migration.go
    case above is unambiguous and requires no such priming assumption).
  - internal/config/config.go SaveUserSettings: still dead code (no call
    sites), attempt-2 comment stands, not itself a new violation of the
    README sentence (it doesn't run), but its existence plus the
    migration.go finding shows the "replace or refuse, cache is the only
    exception" framing keeps needing new asterisks as more of the tree is
    swept — the enumeration strategy itself is not holding up.

Attack 2 (does "refuses to write over it" ever actually happen on a live
path?): age_provider.go's O_EXCL identity-file create is the only literal
"refuse" example found and it is reachable (age/SSH/YubiKey identity setup
flows), so this arm is not vacuous. Not the basis for FAIL.

Attack 3 (re-verify the three code comments against current code):
  - storage.atomicWrite comment: accurately describes the rename-over-a-
    symlink behavior; consistent with observed code and Attack 1's own
    repro semantics for os.Rename vs os.WriteFile.
  - config.SaveUserSettings comment: accurate — still no call sites
    (`grep -rn SaveUserSettings` other than the definition and its own
    doc comment turned up nothing under internal/handlers or cmd).
  - HandlePlotly comment: accurate description of the code immediately
    below it.
  None of the three comments themselves are false. The defect is in the
  README's enumeration, which the comments do not fully cover (migration.go
  has no comment and is not the "cache" the HandlePlotly comment excuses).

Attack 4 (docs-only discipline): `git diff HEAD` touches exactly
README.md, internal/config/config.go, internal/handlers/backup/handlers.go,
internal/services/storage/storage.go, and .swarm/ledger.tsv; all Go changes
are comment-only (no code tokens changed). gofmt -l on the three .go files:
clean. go build ./...: OK. go vet ./...: OK. This discipline point is
satisfied but does not rescue the substantive falsification above.

Attack 5 (symlinked directories / "backups" as "your files"): not reached
as an independent FAIL basis since Attack 1 already succeeds; recorded as
an open question the sentence also does not address (a symlinked directory
under data/, e.g. data/settings -> elsewhere, saves through it transparently
and none of "replaces/refuses/cache exception" describes that case either),
but not adjudicated further here since the migration.go finding is already
sufficient and dispositive.

CONCLUSION: The attempt-3 enumeration ("it either replaces or refuses; the
one exception is data/cache/") is falsified by a live, unconditionally
registered write path (POST /encryption/enable, default password method)
that writes two files directly into the data directory via bare
os.WriteFile, following rather than replacing a symlink at the
destination — reproduced above. FAIL.
